1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,474
This is ECE 320, video 16. The topic is pseudo-LRU replacement, or PLRU. It is unit 8 of the slides.

2
00:00:09,924 --> 00:00:24,134
After this video, you can do four things. You can explain why true LRU is expensive. You can read the PLRU tree bits. You can find the victim on a miss. And you can update the bits on a hit or a fill.

3
00:00:24,584 --> 00:00:42,789
First, recall true LRU from lecture 7. In a 4-way set, each block has an age count. The age goes from 0 to 3, so it needs 2 bits. Here the ages are 1, 3, 0 and 2. This is a row of the example on slide 45.

4
00:00:43,239 --> 00:00:56,428
Now a hit in block 3. Its age becomes 0. All ages lower than its previous age, 2, get plus 1. So blocks 0 and 2 change too. One access changed three age counts.

5
00:00:56,878 --> 00:01:06,073
On a miss, the cache replaces the block with the largest age. Here that is block 1, with age 3. It is the least recently used block.

6
00:01:06,523 --> 00:01:15,764
So true LRU needs 2 bits for each of the 4 blocks. That is 8 bits per set. And every access must compare the age of every block in the set.

7
00:01:16,214 --> 00:01:27,081
Slide 2 of unit 8 says that true LRU is expensive to implement. Count the bits. For 4 ways, the age counts need 4 times 2, which is 8 bits per set.

8
00:01:27,531 --> 00:01:42,717
With N ways, each age goes from 0 to N minus 1. That needs log 2 of N bits per block. So true LRU needs N times log 2 of N bits per set. For 8 ways, that is 24 bits. For 16 ways, it is 64 bits.

9
00:01:43,167 --> 00:01:59,468
PLRU needs only N minus 1 bits per set. Slide 3 gives this rule. That is 3 bits for 4 ways, 7 bits for 8 ways, and 15 bits for 16 ways. For 2 ways, the 1 PLRU bit gives exact LRU.

10
00:01:59,918 --> 00:02:10,506
The work per access is also smaller. Here is an 8-way PLRU tree with some example bit values. It has 7 bits, B0 to B6, in 3 levels.

11
00:02:10,956 --> 00:02:22,566
On a miss, the cache reads only the 3 bits on one path. B0 is 1, so go right. B2 is 1, so go right. B6 is 0, so go left. The victim is way 6.

12
00:02:23,016 --> 00:02:38,381
After the fill of way 6, the cache walks up and writes only the same 3 bits. Each bit points away from way 6. B6 becomes 1, B2 becomes 0, and B0 becomes 0. The other 4 bits do not change.

13
00:02:38,831 --> 00:02:44,822
True LRU must compare all 8 ages on every access, and all of them can change.

14
00:02:45,272 --> 00:02:53,817
This is why the slides call PLRU a hardware friendly approximation of LRU. It uses fewer bits, and its updates are simple.

15
00:02:54,267 --> 00:03:07,967
PLRU is a hardware friendly approximation of LRU. It uses a binary tree to hold the least recently used information of one set. Each node of the tree is one bit. The leaves of the tree are the ways.

16
00:03:08,417 --> 00:03:17,890
On a miss, the cache must find a victim. Only then does it walk the tree. It starts at the root and follows the bits down to one way. That way is the victim.

17
00:03:18,340 --> 00:03:25,492
On a hit, the cache does not walk down. It only updates the PLRU bits on the path from the hit way up to the root.

18
00:03:25,942 --> 00:03:39,317
While it walks up, it sets each bit to point away from the way that was just used. So the way just used cannot be the next victim. This keeps the LRU information. A fill after a miss updates the bits in the same way.

19
00:03:39,767 --> 00:03:57,600
Slide 3 uses a 4-way set-associative cache. The tree needs N minus 1 bits, where N is the number of ways. So 4 ways need 3 bits: B0, B1 and B2. B0 is the root. B1 is above ways 0 and 1. B2 is above ways 2 and 3.

20
00:03:58,050 --> 00:04:05,852
B0 chooses between the two halves of the set. Its left side is ways 0 and 1. Its right side is ways 2 and 3.

21
00:04:06,302 --> 00:04:12,525
B1 chooses between way 0 and way 1. B2 chooses between way 2 and way 3.

22
00:04:12,975 --> 00:04:26,860
The value of a bit tells which side is LRU. 0 means that the left side of the tree is LRU. 1 means that the right side is LRU. The slides write the state as B0, B1, B2 in square brackets.

23
00:04:27,310 --> 00:04:48,812
An example: the state 0 1 1. B0 is 0, so the left half, ways 0 and 1, is LRU. B1 is 1, so way 1 is the LRU way of the left pair. B2 is 1, so way 3 is the LRU way of the right pair. The dashed arrows show where each bit points.

24
00:04:49,262 --> 00:05:08,720
Slide 4 starts with the state 0 0 0. To show the cache set, we give the blocks names. The set holds A, B, C and D in ways 0 to 3. These names are ours. The slides show only the ways. Now block E misses. The cache must replace a block.

25
00:05:09,170 --> 00:05:13,211
Walk the tree from the root. B0 is 0, so go left.

26
00:05:13,661 --> 00:05:22,205
B1 is 0, so go left again. The walk ends at way 0. Way 0 is chosen to be replaced. Block A is evicted.

27
00:05:22,655 --> 00:05:32,129
Block E fills way 0. Now update the state. Walk up from way 0. Way 0 is on the left of B1, so B1 becomes 1, to point right.

28
00:05:32,579 --> 00:05:45,118
Walk up one more level. Way 0 is also on the left of B0, so B0 becomes 1. B2 is not on the path, so it stays 0. The new state is 1 1 0, as on the slide.

29
00:05:45,568 --> 00:05:52,580
Now both bits on the path point away from way 0. So the next miss cannot choose way 0, which holds the newest block.

30
00:05:53,030 --> 00:06:04,130
Slide 5 continues from the state 1 1 0. The set now holds E, B, C and D. Block F misses. Find the victim and the new state. Pause the video and try it.

31
00:06:04,580 --> 00:06:06,762
B0 is 1, so go right.

32
00:06:07,212 --> 00:06:14,782
Now the walk is at B2. B2 is 0, so go left. Way 2 is chosen to be replaced. Block C is evicted.

33
00:06:15,232 --> 00:06:32,090
F fills way 2. Walk up from way 2. Way 2 is on the left of B2, so B2 becomes 1. It is on the right of B0, so B0 becomes 0. B1 is not on the path, so it stays 1. The new state is 0 1 1.

34
00:06:32,540 --> 00:06:40,806
Slide 6 starts with the state 0 1 1. The set holds E, B, F and D. Block G misses. Pause the video and try it.

35
00:06:41,256 --> 00:06:43,578
B0 is 0, so go left.

36
00:06:44,028 --> 00:06:49,554
B1 is 1, so go right. Way 1 is chosen to be replaced. Block B is evicted.

37
00:06:50,004 --> 00:07:03,983
G fills way 1. Way 1 is on the right of B1, so B1 becomes 0. It is on the left of B0, so B0 becomes 1. B2 stays 1. The new state is 1 0 1.

38
00:07:04,433 --> 00:07:13,117
Slide 7 starts with the state 1 0 1. The set holds E, G, F and D. Block H misses. Pause the video and try it.

39
00:07:13,567 --> 00:07:15,750
B0 is 1, so go right.

40
00:07:16,200 --> 00:07:23,584
B2 is 1, so go right. Way 3 is chosen to be replaced. Block D is evicted.

41
00:07:24,034 --> 00:07:36,247
H fills way 3. Way 3 is on the right of B2, so B2 becomes 0. It is on the right of B0, so B0 becomes 0. B1 stays 0. The new state is 0 0 0.

42
00:07:36,697 --> 00:07:47,425
Be careful with the text of slide 7. It writes B1 equals 0, but the bit on the path is B2. The drawing and the result, 0 0 0, are correct.

43
00:07:47,875 --> 00:07:57,859
Slide 8 starts again with the state 0 0 0. The set holds E, G, F and H. Block I misses. Pause the video and try it.

44
00:07:58,309 --> 00:08:00,631
B0 is 0, so go left.

45
00:08:01,081 --> 00:08:08,419
B1 is 0, so go left. Way 0 is chosen to be replaced. Block E is evicted.

46
00:08:08,869 --> 00:08:18,250
I fills way 0. B1 becomes 1, and B0 becomes 1. The new state is 1 1 0. This is the same result as on slide 4.

47
00:08:18,700 --> 00:08:34,025
The text of slide 8 has two typos. It writes B2 where it means B1. And it writes B0 equals 0, B2 equals 1. The drawing shows B0 equals 1 and B1 equals 1. The result on the slide, 1 1 0, is correct.

48
00:08:34,475 --> 00:08:50,729
Put slides 4 to 8 together. The end state of each slide is the start state of the next one. The state goes from 0 0 0 to 1 1 0, 0 1 1 and 1 0 1. Then it is 0 0 0 again. The miss on slide 8 gives 1 1 0.

49
00:08:51,179 --> 00:09:01,210
Five misses in a row replace way 0, way 2, way 1, way 3, and then way 0 again. In 4 misses, each way is replaced once.

50
00:09:01,660 --> 00:09:12,371
Rule 1, for a miss. Start at the root. If the bit is 0, go left. If it is 1, go right. The way at the end of the walk is the victim.

51
00:09:12,821 --> 00:09:32,140
Rule 2, for a hit or a fill. Set each bit on the path to point away from the used way. The table lists the result for each way. For example, the tree shows a use of way 2. B2 becomes 1 and B0 becomes 0. Both bits point away from way 2.

52
00:09:32,590 --> 00:09:42,296
A dash in the table means that the bit is not on the path. It keeps its value. So each access writes exactly 2 bits, and their values depend only on the way number.

53
00:09:42,746 --> 00:09:59,464
Slide 9 shows hits. It starts with the state 0 1 1. This is the state after slide 5, so the set holds E, B, F and D. Block F hits in way 2. Find the new state. Pause the video and try it.

54
00:09:59,914 --> 00:10:07,623
On a hit, the cache does not walk down the tree. It walks up from way 2, and sets each bit on the path to point away from way 2.

55
00:10:08,073 --> 00:10:23,074
B2 must be 1, to point at way 3. It is already 1. B0 must be 0, to point at the left half. It is already 0. So the state does not change: 0 1 1. The slide writes, unchanged.

56
00:10:23,524 --> 00:10:27,750
Next, block E hits in way 0. Pause the video and try it.

57
00:10:28,200 --> 00:10:40,583
Walk up from way 0. B1 must point away from way 0, so B1 must be 1. B0 must point away from the left half, so B0 must be 1.

58
00:10:41,033 --> 00:10:49,670
B1 is already 1. B0 changes from 0 to 1. The new state is 1 1 1, as on the slide.

59
00:10:50,120 --> 00:11:00,105
From 1 1 1, the next miss goes right, and then right again, to way 3. Ways 0 and 2 were just used, and the tree keeps both of them.

60
00:11:00,555 --> 00:11:16,020
Slide 11 is a note about the approximation. It starts with the state 1 0 1. This is the state after slide 6, so the set holds E, G, F and D. Block F hits in way 2. Pause the video and try it.

61
00:11:16,470 --> 00:11:21,294
Walk up from way 2. B2 must be 1, and B0 must be 0.

62
00:11:21,744 --> 00:11:29,964
B2 is already 1. B0 changes from 1 to 0. The new state is 0 0 1, as on the slide.

63
00:11:30,414 --> 00:11:37,984
Then the slide asks for a new replacement. A new block, X, misses. Find the victim. Pause the video and try it.

64
00:11:38,434 --> 00:11:40,756
B0 is 0, so go left.

65
00:11:41,206 --> 00:11:48,544
B1 is 0, so go left. Way 0 is chosen to be replaced. Block E is evicted.

66
00:11:48,994 --> 00:11:55,031
X fills way 0. B0 and B1 become 1. The new state is 1 1 1.

67
00:11:55,481 --> 00:12:06,580
The slide draws the tree 0 0 1 for the new replacement, but it does not show the result. The walk gives way 0. Next, we check if way 0 really holds the least recently used block.

68
00:12:07,030 --> 00:12:21,055
Here is the full history of this set. A, B, C and D fill the set first. Then E, F and G miss, on slides 4, 5 and 6. Then F hits, on slide 11. Call these times t 1 to t 8.

69
00:12:21,505 --> 00:12:31,675
Now look at the last use of each block in the set. E was last used at time 5. G at time 7, F at time 8, and D at time 4.

70
00:12:32,125 --> 00:12:38,152
True LRU replaces the block with the oldest last use. That is D, in way 3.

71
00:12:38,602 --> 00:12:50,816
PLRU walks the tree 0 0 1 and replaces way 0. That is block E. But E was used after D. So here PLRU does not choose the least recently used block.

72
00:12:51,266 --> 00:13:11,607
The reason is that each bit only records which side of its node was used more recently. The hit on F set B0 to point left. But the oldest block, D, is on the right side too, next to F. Three bits cannot hold the full order of four blocks. This is why PLRU is only an approximation of LRU.

73
00:13:12,057 --> 00:13:29,286
Now compare the two policies on the misses of slides 4 to 8. Both sets start with A, B, C and D, used in that order. The bottom line shows this use order, from old to new. For this order, the PLRU state is 0 0 0. That is the start state of slide 4.

74
00:13:29,736 --> 00:13:40,557
E misses. Both policies replace A, in way 0. For true LRU, A is the oldest block, in red. E is now the newest block.

75
00:13:41,007 --> 00:13:52,756
F misses. PLRU walks the tree 1 1 0 and replaces way 2, block C. True LRU replaces its oldest block, B, in way 1. The victims differ.

76
00:13:53,206 --> 00:14:03,376
G misses. PLRU walks the tree 0 1 1 and replaces way 1, block B. True LRU replaces its oldest block, C, in way 2.

77
00:14:03,826 --> 00:14:11,535
H misses. Both policies replace block D, in way 3. D is the oldest block for true LRU.

78
00:14:11,985 --> 00:14:16,536
I misses. Both policies replace block E, in way 0.

79
00:14:16,986 --> 00:14:36,073
Here all accesses miss, so both policies have 5 misses. But between the accesses, they hold different blocks. After F, PLRU still holds B, and true LRU still holds C. A later access to one of these blocks gives a hit in one policy and a miss in the other.

80
00:14:36,523 --> 00:14:54,588
A practice question in exam style. It is not from the slides, but it uses only the slide rules. The state is 1 1 1. Then the set gets a hit on way 3, a miss, a hit on way 1, and a miss. Find each victim and each new state. Pause the video and try it.

81
00:14:55,038 --> 00:15:08,154
Hit on way 3. Walk up. Way 3 is on the right of B2, so B2 becomes 0. It is on the right of B0, so B0 becomes 0. The state is 0 1 0.

82
00:15:08,604 --> 00:15:20,771
Miss. B0 is 0, so go left. B1 is 1, so go right. The victim is way 1. After the fill, B1 becomes 0 and B0 becomes 1. The state is 1 0 0.

83
00:15:21,221 --> 00:15:29,998
Hit on way 1. B1 must be 0, and B0 must be 1. Both bits already have these values. The state stays 1 0 0.

84
00:15:30,448 --> 00:15:44,102
Miss. B0 is 1, so go right. B2 is 0, so go left. The victim is way 2. After the fill, B2 becomes 1 and B0 becomes 0. The state is 0 0 1.

85
00:15:44,552 --> 00:15:57,462
The answer: the victims are way 1 and then way 2. The final state is 0 0 1. The method: on a miss, walk down. On every access, set the 2 bits on the path to point away from the used way.

86
00:15:57,912 --> 00:16:18,345
A second practice question, also not from the slides. The set holds A, B, C and D, used in that order. So the PLRU state is 0 0 0. The true LRU order is A, B, C, D, from oldest to newest. The accesses are A, E, B and F. Do both policies. Pause the video and try it.

87
00:16:18,795 --> 00:16:32,263
A hits in way 0, in both policies. PLRU sets B0 and B1 to 1, so the state is 1 1 0. In true LRU, A becomes the newest block. The oldest block is now B.

88
00:16:32,713 --> 00:16:41,908
E misses. PLRU goes right, then left, to way 2. So C is evicted. True LRU evicts its oldest block, B, in way 1.

89
00:16:42,358 --> 00:16:52,621
B comes back. PLRU still holds B, so it is a hit. True LRU evicted B, so it is a miss. True LRU now evicts C, its oldest block.

90
00:16:53,071 --> 00:17:03,102
F misses in both. PLRU goes right, then right, to way 3. So D is evicted. True LRU also evicts D, its oldest block.

91
00:17:03,552 --> 00:17:15,859
PLRU has 2 misses, and true LRU has 3. So PLRU is not always worse. It is an approximation: it can choose a different victim, and the result can be better or worse.

92
00:17:16,309 --> 00:17:27,315
Here is the recap. One. True LRU with age counts needs N times log 2 of N bits per set. Every access compares all ages. It is expensive.

93
00:17:27,765 --> 00:17:36,867
Two. PLRU uses a binary tree of N minus 1 bits per set. For 4 ways, the bits are B0, B1 and B2.

94
00:17:37,317 --> 00:17:45,661
Three. A bit of 0 means that the left side is LRU. A bit of 1 means that the right side is LRU.

95
00:17:46,111 --> 00:17:54,098
Four. On a miss, walk from the root. Go left on 0 and right on 1. The way at the end is the victim.

96
00:17:54,548 --> 00:18:02,793
Five. On a hit or a fill, set each bit on the path to point away from the used way. The other bits keep their value.

97
00:18:03,243 --> 00:18:10,719
Six. PLRU is an approximation. It can evict a block that is not the true least recently used block, as on slide 11.

98
00:18:11,169 --> 00:18:19,761
Seven. Check the slide text against the drawings. Slides 7 and 8 have typos, but their results are correct. That is the end of video 16.
